---
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArchiveLab from '../../labs/archive/ArchiveLab.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Archive Lab | Keep the originals, rebuild the index" description="Inspect a real isolated Photo Archive index rebuild, then verify three synthetic PNGs in your own browser against their recorded SHA-256 hashes.">
  <main id="main" class="al-page">
    <section class="al-hero wrap" id="top">
      <a class="al-back" href="/labs/"><Icon name="arrow-left" /> All demonstrations</a>
      <div class="al-hero-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Archive Lab / An inspectable engineering proof</p><h1>Keep the originals.<br /><em>Rebuild the index.</em></h1></div><div class="al-intro"><p>A useful archive keeps the original bytes and the record of what they are. Its search index can be built again.</p><p>Here, three generated images put that boundary to a real test. You can also check the files yourself.</p><a href="#al-check-title">Check the three originals <Icon name="arrow-down" /></a></div></div>
    </section>
    <div class="wrap"><ArchiveLab /></div>
    <section class="al-context wrap"><p class="eyebrow">A narrow proof, deliberately</p><h2>Know what<br /><em>the evidence covers.</em></h2><p>This run demonstrates original-byte preservation and reconstruction of a derived SQLite index from canonical manifests. It does not establish a second physical backup, long-term durability, Apple Photos behavior, production archive safety or machine-learning results.</p><p>Photo Archive is the storage boundary within a wider photo platform. This demonstration stays at that boundary.</p><a class="text-link" href="/world/#memory">Explore the memory district <span aria-hidden="true"><Icon name="arrow-up-right" /></span></a></section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
